--TEST--
`#[Json\Derive]` refuses a field whose declared type has no wire form at the property that declared it, not at the `decodeAs<T>` that would later fail — `rule:core-classes/derive-field-list` and `rule:core-classes/derive-generates-what-is-missing`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 2 names the reachable set: "a scalar, an enum, an inline shape, an
// `array<T>` or `?T` of one of those, or another class that itself carries a
// codec". `Handle` is none of those, so the contract `Row` would generate has a
// field nothing can encode — and § 7's rule is that this is said where the
// property is written, because a class is declared once and decoded from many
// call sites.
//
// Both sides of the bound are the same type in the same class, which is what
// makes this more than a restatement of the message. `$cache` is also a
// `Handle`; § 2's own escape hatch takes it off the contract, so it is examined
// and accepted before `$h` is reached. A rule refusing the *type's appearance*
// rather than its presence in the contract would name both properties and still
// look right on the second half alone.
#[Core\Json\Derive]
class Row {
    #[Core\Json\Field(skip: true)]
    public Handle $cache;

    public Handle $h;

    public function constructor(Handle $cache, Handle $h)
    {
        $this->cache = $cache;
        $this->h = $h;
    }
}

// Declared *after* the class that names it: the reachability question is asked
// once the whole program has been walked, so declaration order cannot change
// the answer. Giving `Handle` a `#[Core\Json\Derive]` of its own is what makes
// the same file compile.
class Handle {
    public int $n;

    public function constructor(int $n)
    {
        $this->n = $n;
    }
}

echo "unreachable ", Core\Json::encode(new Row(new Handle(1), new Handle(2))), "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0756]: `Row::$h` is declared `Handle`, which has no JSON representation
   --> case.nvs:20:19
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
